architecture speech-recognition-repair
  canonical_name SpeechRecognitionRepair
  family commons
  domain Commons / Speech recognition
  layer commons
  semantic_question Which recognised segments does the acoustic and token evidence distrust, and does a second recognition of that audio score better than the first?
  purpose The HCP transcriber: recognition, a complex-spectral diagnosis of token confidence that flags segments, and a repair pass that recognises each flagged segment's own audio window again and accepts the candidate only when it scores above the original. It is a different program from the managed bonfyre-transcribe command and is selected by name.
  maturity measured
  source_path components/BonfyreTranscribe/src/main.c
  source_path components/BonfyreTranscribe/Makefile
  source_path components/BonfyreTranscribe/tests/redecode_test.sh
  source_path components/BonfyreControlPlane/native-ingest.mjs
  witness components/BonfyreTranscribe/tests/redecode_test.sh speaks a recording and checks that every re-decoded segment carries a scored candidate, that the accepted count equals the segments marked accepted, that an accepted repair keeps the text it replaced and scored higher, that --no-redecode reports zero, and that a default run re-decodes no more than HCP flagged
  witness components/BonfyreControlPlane/tests/native-ingest.test.mjs selects this program as the hcp recognizer and checks repaired <= re-decoded <= diagnosed in the receipt it returns
  interacts live-semantic-track
  internal_calculus diagnosis: token probabilities are lifted to a complex sequence, filtered in the frequency domain, and tokens whose magnitude or phase moves past threshold are flagged; a segment is a repair candidate when its flagged-token ratio passes threshold
  internal_calculus repair: whisper_full() over the segment's samples padded 200 ms either side, with no preceding-text context and a wider beam; the candidate is scored conf * (1 - no_speech) * min(1, 2.4/compression_ratio) and refused outright on n-gram repetition
  invariant hcp_redecoded_segments counts second recognitions that actually ran; hcp_repaired_segments counts accepted candidates; a flagged segment whose window could not be recognised is counted as failed, never as re-decoded
  invariant an accepted repair keeps the replaced text beside the new one and keeps the original media-clock span
  invariant the transcript, subtitle and text writers read the repaired text, so one acceptance updates every output of that run
  forbidden_inference a flagged segment -> a wrong segment
  forbidden_inference a re-decoded segment -> a repaired one (most candidates are rejected)
  forbidden_inference an accepted repair -> a correct transcript (the candidate outscored the original on the model's own confidence; no reference text was consulted)
  forbidden_inference a higher hcp_quality -> lower word error rate (no word-error measurement against reference transcripts exists here)
  source_path components/BonfyreTranscribe/tests/wer_measure.mjs
  witness 2026-10-03 components/BonfyreTranscribe/tests/wer_measure.mjs, base.en, four spoken passages (99 reference words) at four white-noise levels: re-decoding only flagged segments left word error unchanged at three levels (10.10, 11.11, 13.13 percent) and lowered it at the loudest (15.15 to 13.13); re-decoding every segment lowered it at every level (10.10 to 8.08, 11.11 to 9.09, 13.13 to 11.11, 15.15 to 13.13). No accepted repair raised word error. HCP flagged 0 or 1 of about 8 segments per condition
  forbidden_inference the 2026-10-03 word-error figures -> an accuracy claim for speech in a room (synthetic voice, white noise, 99 words; each change is two words)
  known_gap word error has been measured only on four synthetic passages. The acceptance test did not raise error there, but the diagnosis flagged so few segments that the default run rarely repaired anything; whether HCP flags the segments that are actually wrong has not been measured on human speech with reference transcripts
  source_path components/BonfyreTranscribe/tests/preprocess_measure.mjs
  witness 2026-10-03 components/BonfyreTranscribe/tests/preprocess_measure.mjs, base.en, the same four passages at four white-noise levels, recognised as recorded and after an ffmpeg spectral denoise (afftdn): word error unchanged on clean audio (10.10), lower at moderate noise (13.13 to 12.12, 15.15 to 13.13) and higher at the heaviest (18.18 to 19.19); adding a band-pass made the two noisiest levels worse (16.16, 21.21)
  forbidden_inference the 2026-10-03 denoise figures -> a case for or against WebRTC's audio processing module; that module is not packaged on this machine and was not run, and each difference here is one to three words
  known_gap cleaning the signal before recognition has been measured only with ffmpeg filters on synthetic speech, where it helped a little at moderate noise and hurt at heavy noise. No echo cancellation or gain control was exercised, and nothing in the capture path applies any preprocessing
  known_gap the reference comparison does not normalise numerals, so a spoken "seven thirty" recognised as "7:30" counts as error in every figure above
  known_gap the managed bonfyre-transcribe binary does not carry this path, and this program does not carry the managed one's denoise, speech split or vocabulary; the two are alternatives, not one merged recognizer
  known_gap the witness speaks clean synthetic audio, on which HCP flags little; the repair pass is exercised there through --redecode-all rather than through naturally flagged speech

